How to Design a Menu in Figma
=============================
Designing a menu in Figma means creating a structured navigation system (like top 
bars, sidebars, dropdowns, or mega menus) using frames, components, and layout 
tools.

Below is a simple step-by-step guide.

1. Create a New Frame (Screen)
==============================
Start by creating a frame for your design:
Press F
Choose a device size (Desktop / Mobile)

Example:
Desktop: 1440 × 1024
Mobile: 390 × 844

2. Add a Navigation Bar Structure
=================================
Create a rectangle at the top (for top menu):
Height: 60–80px
Full width

Add layout elements:
Logo (left)
Menu items (center or right)
Buttons (login/signup)

Example menu:
Home | About | Services | Contact

3. Use Auto Layout
==================
Auto Layout helps align items properly:
Select menu items
Press Shift + A
Set spacing (e.g., 20px between items)

Benefits:
Responsive design
Easy alignment
Consistent spacing

4. Add Menu Styles
==================
Customize the menu:
Font (e.g., Inter, Roboto)
Font size (14–18px)
Colors (hover, active, default states)
Icons (optional)

Use:
Fill color for background
Stroke for borders
Hover effects for interaction

5. Create Interactive Menu (Prototype)
======================================
To make it clickable:
Go to Prototype tab
Select menu item
Drag interaction to another frame
Set trigger: “On Click” or “While Hovering”

Example:
Home → Home Screen
About → About Page

6. Create Dropdown or Flyout Menu (Optional)
============================================
Steps:
Create submenu frame
Place it under main menu item
Use “On Hover” interaction

Example:
Services
 ├── Web Design
 ├── App Development
 └── UI/UX Design

7. Create Sidebar Menu (Optional)
=================================
For dashboards:
Add vertical rectangle (left side)
Add menu items stacked vertically
Use icons + text

Example:
Dashboard
Analytics
Settings

8. Use Components
=================
Turn menu items into components:
Select item
Right-click → Create Component

Benefits:
Reusable
Easy updates
Consistent design

9. Add Hover and Active States
==============================
Design different states:
Default
Hover (highlight color)
Active (current page indicator)

10. Test the Prototype
======================
Click “Present” to test:
Navigation flow
Menu interactions
Responsiveness

Summary Workflow
================
Frame → Layout → Auto Layout → Style → Prototype → Test

Final Tip
=========
A good Figma menu design should be:
Simple
Consistent
Easy to navigate
Responsive
